(Bug #51: `a text called caption is label.` - a buffer initializer with no
 cast - stored the buffer's STRUCT pointer in the text slot, so the first
 read of the text was a read of the buffer's 24-byte header. Printing it
 gave the capacity field's low byte as a one-character C string: `@` for a
 64-byte buffer, `A` for a 65-byte one. Silent, stable, and wrong.

 The language owner's ruling is that the cast-free spelling means what
 `as text` means, so all three spellings below print the buffer's bytes.
 The two sizes are the register's own proof that the old answer tracked
 the capacity field rather than memory noise.)

a buffer called label is 64 bytes in size.
append "first" to label.
a text called caption is label.
Print caption.

a buffer called banner is 65 bytes in size.
append "first" to banner.
a text called heading is banner.
Print heading.

(The controls: the cast has been right since #41 and `"{label}"` since
 v0.1.17. All three spellings must now agree, word for word.)
a text called converted is label as text.
Print converted.
a text called interpolated is "{label}".
Print interpolated.

(An empty buffer converts to empty text, not to a header read. On
 origin/main this line printed `[ ]` - a 32-byte capacity is 0x20, which
 is a space, so even the case with no bytes in it was reading the header.)
a buffer called untouched is 32 bytes in size.
a text called blank is untouched.
Print "[{blank}]".
If blank is "", print "an empty buffer makes empty text".
